  /* ---------------- Jade: somewhere to put the bag ----------------
     Eleven towns. She said the number herself, twice, and the thing that
     goes with it is the line from q7 that nobody picked up at the time:
     there is nowhere else in this town that is hers. Not the booth — the
     booth belongs to a nineteen-year-old now and she just fixes things in
     it out of habit. She arrives, she works, she gets a coach.

     So the payoff isn't the return. She already did the return; she
     booked it before she'd even gone, at half four, badly, out loud. The
     payoff is arriving somewhere and having a door and a key and a floor
     to put a bag down on — which has not happened to her once in eleven
     towns, and which is why the whole scene turns on a bag going down.

     It's Claire's room because Claire is away for four days and left a
     key, and because of what's on the wall above the bed: four postcards
     stuck up crooked by a woman who was already leaving. Jade has never
     put anything on a wall anywhere. */
