  /* ---------------- Lily: the room by the sea ----------------
     Her first night was in his room, in her house, with four people
     asleep down the hall — and every scene she has ever had is shaped by
     that: the volume, the listening, the reason in her hands.

     This one is the first room she has ever been in with him that isn't
     hers to run. Take the house away and the thing she is most frightened
     of turns out not to be wanting him. It's being wanted for nothing.

     So the scene isn't "she finally lets go" — she did that upstairs on a
     Tuesday. It's a woman with no job in the room finding out what's left
     of her when the usefulness is gone, and the payoff is a sound she has
     never once let herself make under her own roof. */
  function needsLilyAction3() {
    if (isDemo()) return false;
    return !!state.flags["lily_a3_open"] && !state.flags["casual_lily_a3"];
  }
